Title: A Christmas Carol
Credit: Written by
Author: Charles Dickens
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action Victorian gothic period drama, naturalistic faces, fabric, weather, and spectral light.

FADE IN:

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

Ebenezer Scrooge, a lean elderly man with a pointed nose, red-rimmed eyes, blue thin lips, white brows, and a black frock coat, works beside a tiny fire. Bob Cratchit, thin and pale in a worn dark suit and long white comforter, copies letters in the colder adjoining tank.

(SOUND: quill scratching, distant city bells, fog-muted hoofbeats)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Marley was dead: to begin with.

FRED, Scrooge's ruddy-faced young nephew in a dark green coat, bursts through the office door, breath smoking.

FRED
A merry Christmas, uncle! God save you!

SCROOGE
Bah! Humbug!

FRED
Christmas a humbug, uncle! You don't mean that, I am sure?

SCROOGE
Merry Christmas! What right have you to be merry? What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

FRED
What right have you to be dismal? What reason have you to be morose? You're rich enough.

BOB involuntarily claps once, then quickly pokes his meagre coal into darkness.

(SOUND: poker scraping grate, the last coal hissing out)

SCROOGE
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation!

FRED
Christmas is a kind, forgiving, charitable, pleasant time; when men and women open their shut-up hearts freely.

SCROOGE
Keep Christmas in your own way, and let me keep it in mine.

FRED
Come and dine with us to-morrow.

SCROOGE
Good afternoon.

FRED
I want nothing from you. I ask nothing of you. Why cannot we be friends?

SCROOGE
Good afternoon!

FRED leaves without anger. At the outer door, he warmly shakes BOB'S hand.

MR. HARTLEY, a portly charity collector with silver hair and a respectable wool coat, enters carrying a ledger.

MR. HARTLEY
At this festive season, many thousands are in want of common necessaries.

SCROOGE
Are there no prisons?

MR. HARTLEY
Plenty of prisons.

SCROOGE
And the Union workhouses? Are they still in operation?

MR. HARTLEY
They are. Still, I wish I could say they were not.

SCROOGE
If they would rather die, they had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

MR. HARTLEY closes his ledger, stunned, and withdraws.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hard and sharp as flint, from which no steel had ever struck out generous fire.

EXT. CORNHILL - CHRISTMAS EVE

Fog swallows Victorian London. Shop windows glow over holly, geese, fruit, and puddings. Ragged families warm their hands around a brazier while Scrooge walks past without looking.

(SOUND: wind through alleys, brazier crackle, church bell tolling)

A CHILD CAROLER in a patched brown coat sings at the counting-house keyhole.

CHILD CAROLER
God bless you, merry gentleman! May nothing you dismay!

SCROOGE swings open the door with a ruler raised. The CHILD CAROLER flees into the fog.

(SOUND: ruler striking the doorframe, running footsteps fading)

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

BOB knots his white comforter beneath his chin and reaches for his hat. SCROOGE buttons his greatcoat to the throat.

(SOUND: clock ticking, fog pressing against windowpanes)

SCROOGE
You'll want all day to-morrow, I suppose?

BOB
If quite convenient, sir.

SCROOGE
It's not convenient, and it's not fair.

BOB
It is only once a year, sir.

SCROOGE
A poor excuse for picking a man's pocket every twenty-fifth of December!

SCROOGE locks his cashbox. BOB hurries out toward the Christmas streets.

SCROOGE
Be here all the earlier next morning.

INT. SCROOGE'S CHAMBERS - NIGHT

Scrooge's chambers are a gloomy suite: bare sitting room, iron bed, faded Dutch tiles, a little saucepan of gruel by a low fire. SCROOGE enters through the black hall.

(SOUND: heavy door booming through the empty building)

The brass door knocker twists into the livid face of JACOB MARLEY, Scrooge's dead partner: hollow cheeks, ghostly spectacles on his forehead, white hair stirred by an unseen breath.

SCROOGE blinks. The knocker is ordinary again.

SCROOGE
Pooh, pooh!

He double-locks the door, checks beneath the bed and table, then sits in his faded dressing gown before the fireplace.

(SOUND: spoon tapping basin, fire barely crackling)

The unused bell above him begins to sway.

(SOUND: bell ringing softly, then violently, joined by bells throughout the house)

A chain clanks in the cellar below. It climbs the staircase.

(SOUND: iron chain dragging upward, cellar door booming open)

The door remains locked. MARLEY'S GHOST passes through it, transparent and cold. Steel chains of cashboxes, keys, padlocks, ledgers, deeds, and purses coil around his body.

(SOUND: chain crashing against floorboards, fire flaring)

SCROOGE
What do you want with me?

MARLEY
Much.

SCROOGE
Who are you?

MARLEY
Ask me who I was.

SCROOGE
Who were you then?

MARLEY
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

SCROOGE
You may be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a crumb of cheese.

MARLEY raises a terrible cry and unwraps the bandage around his jaw. His jaw drops upon his breast.

(SOUND: spectral cry, teeth clicking, chain shrieking)

SCROOGE falls to his knees.

SCROOGE
Mercy! Dreadful apparition, why do you trouble me?

MARLEY
I wear the chain I forged in life. I made it link by link, and yard by yard.

SCROOGE
You were always a good man of business, Jacob.

MARLEY
Business! Mankind was my business. Charity, mercy, forbearance, and benevolence were all my business.

MARLEY lifts his chain. The heavy links fall through the room.

(SOUND: steel links striking wood, wind moaning in chimney)

MARLEY
You will be haunted by Three Spirits.

SCROOGE
I think I'd rather not.

MARLEY
Expect the first when the bell tolls One.

SCROOGE
Could I take them all at once, and have it over, Jacob?

MARLEY
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread.

MARLEY walks backward. The window rises by itself.

(SOUND: window sash groaning open, distant wailing)

Outside, chained phantoms drift over the dark city, reaching helplessly toward people below.

MARLEY
Look that, for your own sake, you remember what has passed between us.

MARLEY floats into the night. SCROOGE stares after him as the phantoms dissolve into fog.

(SOUND: chains receding, winter wind swallowing the cries)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

SCROOGE lies rigidly in his bed, still in his dressing gown. Frost veils the window. The clock hands reach one.

(SOUND: church clock striking one, rapid heartbeat)

A white hand pulls open his bed curtains. THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PAST stands close: child-sized yet ancient, with white hair, smooth face, bare feet, white tunic, holly branch, and a beam of clear light from its crown.

(SOUND: soft chime shimmering beneath the clock's final toll)

SCROOGE
Are you the Spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am.

SCROOGE
Who, and what are you?

CHRISTMAS PAST
I am the Ghost of Christmas Past.

SCROOGE
Long Past?

CHRISTMAS PAST
No. Your past.

CHRISTMAS PAST takes SCROOGE'S arm and leads him toward the window.

CHRISTMAS PAST
Your reclamation. Take heed!

EXT. COUNTRY ROAD - DAY

A clear winter day replaces the fog. Snow rests on hedges and fields. Young boys race home in carts and on shaggy ponies, calling Merry Christmas.

(SOUND: pony hooves, boys shouting, crisp wind over snow)

SCROOGE, still elderly and black-coated, recognizes every gate and tree. His eyes shine with tears.

SCROOGE
I was bred in this place. I was a boy here!

CHRISTMAS PAST
These are but shadows of the things that have been.

EXT. BOARDING SCHOOL YARD - DAY

A decayed red-brick school stands isolated beneath bare trees. YOUNG EBENEZER, a thin solemn boy with dark hair and patched school clothes, reads alone by a weak window fire.

(SOUND: wind rattling broken panes, mice scuffling behind walls)

SCROOGE watches his younger self through the window.

SCROOGE
Poor boy!

A fantasy from the book comes alive around YOUNG EBENEZER: ALI BABA leads an ass through a snowy imagined desert; a bright parrot flaps past the schoolroom window.

(SOUND: pages turning, faint imagined adventure music)

SCROOGE
It's dear old honest Ali Baba! Poor Robin Crusoe, where have you been?

The school door bursts open. FAN, a delicate little girl with chestnut curls, a blue wool cape, and bright eager eyes, runs to YOUNG EBENEZER.

(SOUND: school door banging, Fan's quick footsteps)

FAN
I have come to bring you home, dear brother! Home, for good and all. Home, for ever and ever!

YOUNG EBENEZER
Home, little Fan?

FAN
Father is so much kinder than he used to be. We're to be together all the Christmas long.

SCROOGE presses his gloved hand over his mouth as YOUNG EBENEZER and FAN leave together.

CHRISTMAS PAST
She had a large heart.

SCROOGE
So she had. God forbid that I should gainsay it.

CHRISTMAS PAST
She died a woman, and had one child.

SCROOGE
My nephew.

INT. FEZZIWIG'S WAREHOUSE - CHRISTMAS EVE

The warehouse glows with lamps and a roaring fire. FEZZIWIG, a plump elderly man in a Welsh wig and broad waistcoat, claps his hands. YOUNG EBENEZER, now a slim young apprentice in a brown waistcoat, and DICK WILKINS raise the shutters.

(SOUND: shutters clattering, fiddle tuning, laughter gathering)

FEZZIWIG
Yo ho, there! Ebenezer! Dick! No more work to-night. Christmas Eve!

The warehouse transforms into a ballroom. MRS. FEZZIWIG, round-faced and rosy in a plum silk gown, dances with FEZZIWIG as apprentices whirl around them.

(SOUND: lively fiddle playing “Sir Roger de Coverley,” dancing shoes stomping)

SCROOGE laughs with his former self, then looks suddenly stricken.

CHRISTMAS PAST
A small matter, to make these silly folks so full of gratitude.

SCROOGE
Small! He has the power to render us happy or unhappy; to make our service light or burdensome.

SCROOGE watches FEZZIWIG shake each departing apprentice's hand.

SCROOGE
I should like to be able to say a word or two to my clerk just now. That's all.

INT. BELLE'S PARLOUR - NIGHT

A modest parlour glows with a winter fire. BELLE, a gentle young woman with dark braided hair, a plain mourning dress, and steady grey eyes, sits opposite YOUNG EBENEZER. A coin glints in his tense hand.

(SOUND: fire popping, a clock ticking heavily)

BELLE
Another idol has displaced me.

YOUNG EBENEZER
What idol has displaced you?

BELLE
A golden one.

YOUNG EBENEZER
There is nothing on which the world is so hard as poverty.

BELLE
You fear the world too much. Your nobler aspirations have fallen off one by one, until Gain engrosses you.

YOUNG EBENEZER looks away from her.

BELLE
If this had never been between us, would you seek me out and try to win me now?

YOUNG EBENEZER cannot answer.

(SOUND: clock chime, fire settling)

BELLE
I release you. With a full heart, for the love of him you once were.

BELLE exits. CHRISTMAS PAST turns SCROOGE toward a second vision in the same room, years later: BELLE, older but still dark-haired and gentle, sits amid laughing children while her husband comes home.

(SOUND: children shrieking happily, front door opening)

BELLE'S HUSBAND
I passed his office window. His partner lies upon the point of death, and there he sat alone.

BELLE'S HUSBAND
Quite alone in the world, I do believe.

SCROOGE grips the Spirit's white sleeve.

SCROOGE
Remove me from this place. I cannot bear it!

CHRISTMAS PAST'S light brightens. SCROOGE seizes its extinguisher-cap and forces it down.

(SOUND: rushing light, SCROOGE'S ragged breath)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

SCROOGE collapses across his bed. A red glow spills from the adjoining sitting room.

(SOUND: clock striking one, deep fire roaring beyond the door)

INT. SCROOGE'S SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

The bare sitting room has become a Christmas grove. Holly and ivy cover the walls. A feast of geese, turkeys, puddings, fruit, chestnuts, and steaming punch surrounds a throne.

(SOUND: logs roaring, chestnuts cracking, distant bells)

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS PRESENT sits there: a towering broad-chested giant with long dark brown curls, genial eyes, a green fur-trimmed robe, holly wreath, and antique empty scabbard.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Come in! Come in, and know me better, man!

SCROOGE
I am the Ghost of Christmas Present?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Look upon me.

SCROOGE
Conduct me where you will. If you have aught to teach me, let me profit by it.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT rises and offers his green robe.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Touch my robe.

EXT. LONDON MARKET STREET - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow lies in deep cart-ruts. Londoners laugh while scraping pavements. Bakers, grocers, and poulterers shine with food and warm light.

(SOUND: shovels scraping snow, market calls, church bells ringing)

CHRISTMAS PRESENT sprinkles light from his torch over a quarrelling pair of dinner-carriers. Their anger breaks into embarrassed laughter.

SCROOGE
Is there a peculiar flavour in what you sprinkle from your torch?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
There is. My own.

SCROOGE
Would it apply to any kind of dinner on this day?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
To any kindly given. To a poor one most.

EXT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - CHRISTMAS DAY

A narrow, worn house stands in a snowy Camden Town lane. CHRISTMAS PRESENT blesses the door with his torch while SCROOGE watches.

(SOUND: children laughing inside, kettle singing, wind around the door)

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - CHRISTMAS DAY

The small room is crowded but bright. MRS. CRATCHIT, a tired but lively woman in a twice-turned gown with red ribbons, lays the table. PETER wears Bob's oversized shirt collar. MARTHA hides behind a door.

(SOUND: potatoes bubbling, plates clinking, children whispering)

BOB enters in his threadbare coat and white comforter, carrying TINY TIM on his shoulder. TINY TIM is a pale little boy with brown curls, a small crutch, and iron leg braces.

BOB
Why, where's our Martha?

MRS. CRATCHIT
Not coming.

BOB'S smile fades. MARTHA leaps out and embraces him.

(SOUND: delighted squeals, Tiny Tim's crutch tapping floor)

BOB settles TINY TIM by the fire.

MRS. CRATCHIT
And how did little Tim behave?

BOB
As good as gold, and better. He hoped the people saw him in church, because he was a cripple.

BOB
It might be pleasant to them to remember who made lame beggars walk, and blind men see.

The family carries in a goose. MRS. CRATCHIT carves it. Everyone leans forward.

(SOUND: carving knife through crisp skin, one collective gasp)

TINY TIM
Hurrah!

The pudding arrives blazing with brandy and holly.

(SOUND: brandy flaming, children cheering, spoon against bowls)

BOB raises a small cup.

BOB
A merry Christmas to us all, my dears. God bless us!

CRATCHIT CHILDREN
God bless us!

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

SCROOGE looks at TINY TIM'S fragile hand in BOB'S.

SCROOGE
Tell me if Tiny Tim will live.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
I see a vacant seat in the poor chimney-corner, and a crutch without an owner.

SCROOGE
No, no. Oh, no, kind Spirit! Say he will be spared.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If these shadows remain unaltered by the Future, the child will die.

SCROOGE bows his head.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
If he be like to die, he had better do it, and decrease the surplus population.

(SOUND: fire crackling abruptly louder, Scrooge's sharp intake of breath)

MRS. CRATCHIT reluctantly raises her cup.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I'll drink his health for your sake and the Day's, not for his.

BOB
My dear. Christmas Day.

MRS. CRATCHIT
Long life to him! A merry Christmas and a happy new year!

The family drinks. SCROOGE cannot meet their eyes.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

FRED and his wife CLARA, a pretty young woman with dark curls, a blue silk dress, and bright laughing eyes, entertain friends around a fire. Dessert and mulled wine cover the table.

(SOUND: warm laughter, glasses clinking, harp music)

FRED
He said that Christmas was a humbug, as I live!

CLARA
More shame for him, Fred!

FRED
His wealth is of no use to him. He does no good with it. He does not make himself comfortable with it.

FRED laughs so hard he must clutch the mantel.

(SOUND: Fred's infectious laughter spreading around the room)

FRED
I am sorry for him. Who suffers by his ill whims? Himself, always.

SCROOGE smiles despite himself.

SCROOGE
I should like to stay, Spirit. One half hour only.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT gestures toward a game of Yes and No.

CLARA
Is it an animal?

FRED
Yes.

CLARA
Does it live in London?

FRED
Yes.

CLARA
Does it growl and grunt sometimes?

FRED
Yes!

CLARA
I know what it is! It's your Uncle Scro-o-o-o-oge!

The room erupts with laughter. SCROOGE almost joins it.

(SOUND: applause, laughter, fire snapping)

FRED
He has given us plenty of merriment. Here is a glass of mulled wine. Uncle Scrooge!

ALL
Uncle Scrooge!

SCROOGE raises an invisible glass.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas and a happy New Year to you all.

EXT. LONDON STREET - MIDNIGHT

The CHRISTMAS PRESENT has grown old and grey. Snow whips around his green robe. Two starving children crawl from beneath its folds: IGNORANCE, a gaunt boy in torn clothes, and WANT, a hollow-eyed girl in filthy rags.

(SOUND: midnight bell tolling, bitter wind, distant hungry coughing)

SCROOGE recoils.

SCROOGE
Spirit! Are they yours?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
They are Man's. This boy is Ignorance. This girl is Want.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT points toward IGNORANCE.

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Beware them both, but most of all beware this boy, for on his brow I see that written which is Doom.

SCROOGE
Have they no refuge or resource?

CHRISTMAS PRESENT
Are there no prisons? Are there no workhouses?

The last bell stroke fades. CHRISTMAS PRESENT vanishes. A tall hooded shape glides through the snow.

(SOUND: bell vibration dying into silence, low funeral drone)

EXT. LONDON EXCHANGE - DAY

THE GHOST OF CHRISTMAS YET TO COME stands tall in a deep black hooded garment. Only one outstretched hand is visible. Merchants cluster beneath the Exchange portico.

(SOUND: coins chinking, carriage wheels, cold wind)

SCROOGE follows the hooded phantom, trembling.

SCROOGE
Ghost of the Future, I fear you more than any spectre I have seen. Lead on.

MR. POOLE, a fat merchant with a huge chin, speaks to MR. SNIPE, a red-faced trader with a gold seal.

MR. POOLE
I only know he's dead.

MR. SNIPE
When did he die?

MR. POOLE
Last night, I believe.

MR. SNIPE
What has he done with his money?

MR. POOLE
Left it to his company, perhaps. He has not left it to me.

The merchants laugh and walk away.

(SOUND: their laughter swallowed by traffic)

SCROOGE searches the crowd for himself, but his usual office window shows another man in his chair.

INT. OLD JOE'S SHOP - NIGHT

A low, grimy shop is piled with rusty keys, chains, rags, bones, scales, and bottles. OLD JOE, grey-haired and nearly seventy, smokes by a charcoal stove.

(SOUND: charcoal hissing, shop hinges screeching, rain tapping roof)

MRS. DILBER, a hard-faced charwoman in a stained bonnet, enters with a bundle. MRS. RIGGS, a laundress with a shawl and rough red hands, follows. TOM BAXTER, an undertaker's assistant in faded black, brings small valuables.

MRS. DILBER
What odds then! Every person has a right to take care of themselves. He always did.

OLD JOE chalks prices onto the wall for TOM'S seals, pencils, buttons, and brooch.

(SOUND: chalk squeaking, coins dropped onto a counter)

MRS. RIGGS opens her bundle. Bed-curtains spill out.

OLD JOE
Bed-curtains!

MRS. RIGGS
Yes I do. Why not?

OLD JOE
You took them down, rings and all, with him lying there?

MRS. RIGGS
If he had been natural in his lifetime, he'd have had somebody to look after him when he was struck with Death.

MRS. DILBER
It's a judgment on him.

MRS. RIGGS
He frightened every one away from him when he was alive, to profit us when he was dead!

They laugh over their coins. SCROOGE stares in horror.

(SOUND: harsh laughter, coins rattling, storm gust forcing the door)

SCROOGE
The case of this unhappy man might be my own. My life tends that way now.

INT. DEAD MAN'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A bare uncurtained bed stands in a dark, stripped room. A covered corpse lies alone beneath a ragged sheet. A cat claws at the door; rats gnaw under the hearthstone.

(SOUND: cat scratching wood, rats gnawing, winter wind through cracked glass)

THE GHOST points to the corpse's covered face. SCROOGE reaches toward it but cannot lift the sheet.

SCROOGE
If there is any person in the town who feels emotion caused by this man's death, show that person to me.

EXT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

Snow falls softly over the same narrow house. BOB, still thin and pale in his patched dark coat and long white comforter, walks toward the door with shoulders bowed.

(SOUND: slow footsteps on snow, distant church bell)

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

The room is painfully quiet. TINY TIM'S crutch leans ownerless in the chimney-corner. MRS. CRATCHIT and the children sew by the fire.

(SOUND: needle through cloth, clock ticking, fire whispering)

PETER reads from a book.

PETER
And He took a child, and set him in the midst of them.

BOB enters. The children press close to him.

MRS. CRATCHIT
I have known him walk with Tiny Tim upon his shoulder, very fast indeed.

BOB
And so have I. Often.

BOB goes upstairs.

(SOUND: stair creaking under Bob's slow steps, a muffled sob)

INT. TINY TIM'S BEDROOM - DAY

A small chair sits beside TINY TIM'S neat bed. Christmas holly remains on the wall. BOB sits, bends over the still little face, and kisses it.

(SOUND: Bob's breath catching, wind brushing the window)

BOB
My little, little child.

He composes himself and returns downstairs.

INT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - DAY

BOB rejoins his family. The vacant crutch remains visible beside the fire.

(SOUND: kettle beginning to tremble, quiet crying)

BOB
When we recollect how patient and mild he was, we shall not quarrel easily among ourselves.

CRATCHIT CHILDREN
Never, father!

BOB
I am very happy. I am very happy.

SCROOGE turns from the family toward the waiting black phantom.

SCROOGE
Tell me what man that was whom we saw lying dead.

EXT. CHURCHYARD - NIGHT

A neglected churchyard lies walled by houses. Weeds cover sunken stones. THE GHOST stands beside a grave and points down.

(SOUND: bare branches creaking, midnight wind, a distant raven)

SCROOGE approaches the stone. His hands shake over its carved name: EBENEZER SCROOGE.

(SOUND: low funeral drum, Scrooge falling to his knees)

SCROOGE
Am I that man who lay upon the bed?

The black finger points from the grave to SCROOGE and back again.

SCROOGE
No, Spirit! Oh, no, no!

SCROOGE clutches the Spirit's robe.

SCROOGE
I am not the man I was. I will not be the man I must have been but for this intercourse.

SCROOGE
I will honour Christmas in my heart, and try to keep it all the year.

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future. Tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone!

The hooded hand trembles. The phantom collapses into a bedpost.

(SOUND: wind rising to a roar, graveyard vanishing in a rushing crack)

INT. SCROOGE'S BEDROOM - CHRISTMAS MORNING

SCROOGE wakes in his own bed, clutching the bedpost. He is still the lean old man with white brows and nightcap, but tears shine on his cheeks.

(SOUND: jubilant church bells, bright morning birds)

SCROOGE
I will live in the Past, the Present, and the Future!

He leaps up, laughing and crying. He throws open the window. Clear sunlight fills the room.

(SOUND: window flying open, bells pealing across London)

SCROOGE
What's to-day, my fine fellow?

A BOY in Sunday clothes looks up from the street.

BOY
To-day! Why, Christmas Day.

SCROOGE
It's Christmas Day! I haven't missed it!

SCROOGE
Do you know the Poulterer's, in the next street but one?

BOY
I should hope I did.

SCROOGE
Go and buy the big prize Turkey. Tell them to bring it here.

SCROOGE
Come back with the man in less than five minutes, and I'll give you half-a-crown!

The BOY runs off. SCROOGE pats the ordinary brass knocker affectionately.

(SOUND: boy's boots racing over cobbles, Scrooge's great laughter)

EXT. LONDON STREET - CHRISTMAS DAY

SCROOGE, now dressed in his best black frock coat and silk hat, walks among laughing Londoners with a delighted smile. He sees MR. HARTLEY approaching with his ledger.

(SOUND: carols from distant windows, horse hooves, cheerful market chatter)

SCROOGE takes MR. HARTLEY'S hands.

SCROOGE
My dear sir, I hope you succeeded yesterday. It was very kind of you. A merry Christmas to you, sir!

MR. HARTLEY
Mr. Scrooge?

SCROOGE leans in and whispers an amount into MR. HARTLEY'S ear.

(SOUND: street sounds falling away beneath Hartley's shocked breath)

MR. HARTLEY
My dear Mr. Scrooge, are you serious?

SCROOGE
Not a farthing less. A great many back-payments are included in it.

MR. HARTLEY
I don't know what to say to such munificence.

SCROOGE
Don't say anything, please. Come and see me.

MR. HARTLEY grips SCROOGE'S hand with tears in his eyes.

INT. FRED'S DINING ROOM - CHRISTMAS NIGHT

The dining table is laid for a feast. FRED and CLARA inspect the place settings. SCROOGE opens the door and nervously peers inside.

(SOUND: cutlery arranged, fire crackling, distant guests arriving)

SCROOGE
Fred!

FRED turns, astonished.

FRED
Why bless my soul! Who's that?

SCROOGE
It's I. Your uncle Scrooge. I have come to dinner. Will you let me in, Fred?

FRED strides forward and seizes SCROOGE in an embrace.

(SOUND: chairs scraping, joyous laughter, glassware chiming)

FRED
Let you in? Uncle Scrooge!

CLARA embraces SCROOGE. The room fills with friends, music, and laughter as SCROOGE takes his place among them.

INT. SCROOGE AND MARLEY COUNTING-HOUSE - MORNING

The office is cold but bright. SCROOGE sits waiting with his door open. His white brows, pointed nose, black coat, and wiry chin are unchanged, but his eyes are warm.

(SOUND: clock ticking toward nine, coal scuttle rattling in the court)

BOB slips in eighteen minutes late, hat and comforter already off. He hurries to his stool and takes up his quill.

SCROOGE
Hallo! What do you mean by coming here at this time of day?

BOB freezes.

BOB
I am very sorry, sir. I am behind my time.

SCROOGE
You are? Step this way, sir, if you please.

BOB approaches warily. SCROOGE rises and gives him a hearty dig in the waistcoat.

(SOUND: Bob stumbling back, Scrooge's sudden laugh)

SCROOGE
I am not going to stand this sort of thing any longer.

BOB reaches uncertainly toward the ruler.

SCROOGE
And therefore I am about to raise your salary!

BOB stares.

SCROOGE
A merry Christmas, Bob! A merrier Christmas than I have given you for many a year!

SCROOGE opens the coal-box and fills BOB'S fire himself.

(SOUND: coal spilling into grate, flames catching strongly)

SCROOGE
Make up the fires, and buy another coal-scuttle before you dot another i, Bob Cratchit!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Scrooge was better than his word. To Tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

EXT. CRATCHIT HOUSE - CHRISTMAS DAY

TINY TIM, still pale but smiling, walks between BOB and SCROOGE with his little crutch. SCROOGE, lean and white-browed in his black coat, steadies the child with one gloved hand.

(SOUND: family laughter, crutch tapping pavement, Christmas bells)

TINY TIM
God bless us every one!

FADE OUT.
